---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import TableOfContents from '../components/TableOfContents.astro';
---

<Layout title="Press" description="A brief introduction to Code198x: its purpose, current curriculum, people, evidence standards and contact details.">
  <article class="page">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">Press and enquiries</p>
      <h1>Understand the machine.<br />Make something with it.</h1>
      <p class="lead">Code198x is developing a games development and programming curriculum
        that uses vintage computers to make the workings of software understandable.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="reading-layout">
      <div class="reading-body">
        <section aria-labelledby="brief">
          <h2 id="brief">A short description</h2>
          <div class="brief">
            <p>Code Like It’s 198x teaches programming and game development through vintage
              computers. Readers build games, investigate how the machine works and connect
              that understanding to the choices they make with modern tools. The lessons are
              free to read, with an accompanying encyclopaedia, reusable programming patterns
              and open-source development tools.</p>
          </div>
          <p>You are welcome to use this description when introducing the project.{' '}
            <a href="/about">About Code198x</a> explains the purpose in more depth.</p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="purpose">
          <h2 id="purpose">Why vintage machines?</h2>
          <p>Small programs and visible constraints make it possible to connect a game’s
            rules, graphics and sound to its data and execution. Readers can ask what a
            variable represents, why an animation stutters or how a program talks to a device,
            then investigate a concrete example.</p>
          <p>The purpose is to understand and create. Historical sources help explain the
            choices people made, while comparisons between systems show what transfers and
            what changes. Modern relevance comes through specific questions about responsiveness,
            representation, algorithms and limited resources.</p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="available">
          <h2 id="available">What readers can explore</h2>
          <div class="cards">
            <div class="card"><h3>Programming and games</h3><p><a href="/foundations">Foundations</a>{' '}
              uses language-neutral pseudocode. The initial system focus is the ZX Spectrum,
              Commodore 64, Commodore Amiga and NES. Their{' '}
              <a href="/systems">system pages</a> lead to the available tracks and lessons.</p></div>
            <div class="card"><h3>Context and techniques</h3><p>The <a href="/vault">Vault</a>{' '}
              connects machines, games, people and computing history. The{' '}
              <a href="/patterns">Pattern Library</a> offers concrete techniques to adapt,
              with their machine requirements and trade-offs.</p></div>
          </div>
          <p>The curriculum is in development. A listed machine is not a completed course.
            The Craft’s current Maths for Games and Game Feel pages are outlines for further
            teaching material. Wider system coverage and projects using expansions are part
            of the intended direction, not claims that those lessons already exist.</p>
          <p><a href="/curriculum">The curriculum map</a> explains the organisation.{' '}
            <a href="/updates">What’s New</a> records published additions; dates and claims in
            coverage should refer to the material available at the time.</p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="people">
          <h2 id="people">People and tools</h2>
          <p>Code198x was started by Steve Hill, a software engineer. Contributors can help
            with teaching, code, research, illustration and testing. The{' '}
            <a href="/contribute">contribution page</a> points to scoped work, including
            tasks that welcome review reports without requiring code changes.</p>
          <p>The wider <a href="/family">198x family</a> develops tools for assembly, builds,
            emulation and working with vintage media. These projects have distinct scopes;
            tool support for a machine does not establish curriculum coverage.</p>
          <p>AI assists development and drafting. Claims still need appropriate sources and
            code needs appropriate checks. <a href="/standards">Sources &amp; Standards</a>{' '}
            distinguishes proposals, source checks, builds and observed execution. It does
            not claim that every page or program has been verified on original hardware.</p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="access">
          <h2 id="access">Access and reuse</h2>
          <p>The lessons are free to read. Original prose is available under{}
            <a href="/standards#licence">CC BY 4.0</a>, with attribution and the required notices.
            The maintained code samples are published under
            the <a href="https://github.com/code198x/code-samples/blob/main/LICENSE">MIT licence</a>.
            Check the licence and attribution attached to material you wish to reuse;
            third-party images and software have their own terms.</p>
          <p>Some activities require tools or system ROMs. Requirements depend on the chosen
            language and machine; see <a href="/setup">setup guidance</a>. Free access to the
            curriculum does not mean every target’s software or hardware is supplied.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="marks" aria-labelledby="marks-title">
          <h2 id="marks-title">Using our name and logo</h2>
          <p>Use <strong>Code198x</strong> or <strong>Code Like It’s 198x</strong> when referring
            to the project. You do not need to ask permission to refer to us in an article,
            talk, video, reading list or account of something you built or taught.</p>
          <p>You may reproduce the logo unaltered to identify the project. Do not recolour,
            restyle or crop it, use it as your own branding, or imply endorsement, partnership
            or approval. For other uses, contact us.</p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="contact">
          <h2 id="contact">Contact and further information</h2>
          <p>For interviews, questions or image requests, contact Steve through{' '}
            <a href="https://discord.gg/AdmEYQKWZb">the Code198x Discord</a>. Include the
            publication or event, subject and deadline. Please do not post confidential
            information in a public channel.</p>
          <p>Find the repositories on <a href="https://github.com/code198x">GitHub</a>,
            follow <a href="/updates">project updates</a>, or read{' '}
            <a href="/field-notes">development stories</a>. Technical and historical corrections
            are welcome through the <a href="/standards#corrections">correction routes</a>.</p>
        </section>
      </div>
      <aside><TableOfContents selector=".reading-body" /></aside>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page { max-width: var(--container-max-width); margin: 0 auto; padding: var(--space-10) var(--gutter) var(--space-16); }
  header { margin-bottom: var(--space-10); }
  .eyebrow { font-family: var(--font-family-sans); font-size: var(--h-text-small); color: var(--h-ink-muted); }
  h1 { font-family: var(--font-family-read); font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 5vw, 3.6rem); line-height: 1.08; color: var(--h-ink); margin: var(--space-3) 0 var(--space-6); }
  .lead { font-size: 1.15rem; }
  .reading-layout { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 240px; gap: var(--space-12); align-items: start; }
  .reading-body { min-width: 0; }
  aside { position: sticky; top: 7rem; }
  section { margin-top: var(--space-10); scroll-margin-top: 7rem; }
  section:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
  h2, h3 { font-family: var(--font-family-sans); color: var(--h-ink); line-height: 1.3; }
  h2 { font-size: 1.4rem; } h3 { font-size: 1.1rem; margin-top: 0; }
  p { font-family: var(--font-family-read); max-width: 70ch; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--h-ink-body); margin: var(--space-4) 0; }
  a { color: inherit; text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: .15em; }
  .brief { border-left: 3px solid var(--h-ink); padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-5); background: var(--color-surface); }
  .cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: var(--space-5); }
  .card { border: 1px solid var(--color-border); background: var(--color-surface); padding: var(--space-5); }
  .card p { font-size: var(--h-text-small); margin-bottom: 0; }
  @media (max-width: 1100px) { .reading-layout { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } aside { display: none; } }
  @media (max-width: 680px) { .cards { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
</style>
